      "hints": [
        "Differentiate $\\frac{dV}{dr} = 12\\pi r - \\pi r^2 = \\pi r(12 - r) = 0 \\implies r = 12$.",
        "Show $\\frac{d^2V}{dr^2} = 12\\pi - 2\\pi(12) = -12\\pi < 0 \\implies$ Maximum."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Differentiate $V$ with respect to $r$ and set equal to zero:\n$$\\frac{dV}{dr} = 12\\pi r - \\pi r^2 = \\pi r(12 - r) = 0$$\nSince $r$ is non-zero, $r = 12\\text{ cm}$.",
        "Differentiate a second time to determine the nature of the stationary point:\n$$\\frac{d^2V}{dr^2} = 12\\pi - 2\\pi r$$\n$$\\left.\\frac{d^2V}{dr^2}\\right|_{r=12} = 12\\pi - 2\\pi(12) = -12\\pi < 0$$\nSince the second derivative is strictly negative, the stationary value is a **maximum**."
      ],
